WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.628
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.555 --> 00:00:06.450
The way we walk can tell us
just as much about our health,

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.790
as our pulse or blood pressure.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.430
Professor Katabi's
group at MIT CSAIL

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.550
can now measure a person's
walking speed continuously

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.530
and non-intrusively
with a device

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.740
called WiGait by sending
low radiation wireless

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.560
signals that reflect
off of a person's body

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.130
and back to the device.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.990
WiGait can measure different
people's walking speeds

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.530
with 95% to 99% accuracy.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.300
WiGait could help detect
injuries or health issues,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.330
like cognitive decline
or cardiac disease

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.910
and does so while anonymizing
your data as nothing

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.900
more than a dot on a screen.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.220
WiGait first distinguishes
walking from other movements,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.520
like reading or cleaning and
then uses a special signal

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.680
processing algorithms
to distinguish

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.050
between different
walking speeds.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.220
WiGait is also 85% to
99% accurate at measuring

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.250
a person's stride-length
which could allow researchers

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.670
to better understand conditions
like Parkinson's disease

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.240
that are characterized
by reduced step size.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.000
In the future, the researchers
hope to test the system

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.150
on people with disabilities
like Parkinson's, multiple

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.040
sclerosis, or Alzheimer's.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

